
PART 1 - FEATURES/LABELS


1. Features in my data - As part of the fraud detection AI, the features are the normal (median)
transaction price for a customer, the distance from the last completed transaction, and the distance
from the customer's home. The other two are whether or not it was somewhere the customer has shopped already,
and if the order was online or not. Those are not as critical as the others, but can still make some impact 
on detecting fraud. 

2. Label for data - The model is trying to predict if fraud is occurring, It is a classification label
because it is choosing between multiple things, not predicting a value. It also uses supervised learning because
historical customer data is the best indicator of seeing what fraud looks like on a customer account. 


PART 2 - MODEL OUTCOMES


3. Training data - Training data contains a majority (but not all) of the available historical 
customer data for the model to learn patterns from. The model will examine about 80 percent of the available data 
and attempt to draw conclusions about exactly what patterns make it suspect that fraud is occurring. It will not simply 
memorize certain thresholds that immediately trigger fraud detection, but instead look at general trends to be able to 
predict it in as many unique circumstances as possible. The model is useless if it can only predict the historical data and 
not any new data. 

4. Testing data - The testing data needs to be separate from the training to see if the model can 
correctly predict the testing data after it has been trained. This is the test that determines if the model has actually 
learned patterns in the data being given and can identify predictions for data it has never seen before. If the model has 
just memorized the training data and not learned anything from it, the model will fail to do anything productive when given
new data. 

5. False positive - A false positive is when the model identifies something as positive, but it is wrong and the 
actual result should be negative. In this case that would be the model identifying fraud when none is occurring. 
Based on the model's features, this could happen when a customer purchases something high above their median price, or if they happen to 
be on vacation far from their home address. The AI would flag these as abnormal, and while they are, this is not always a guarantee of fraud.

6. False negative - A false negative is when the AI predicts the result as negative, but the actual result should be 
positive. In this case that would be the model being unable to identify when fraud is occurring. Since this model
is analyzing normal customer transactions on their accounts, it would fail to detect fraud if there was no spike in abnormal activity on
the account. Essentially, because there is only these five features, if the criminal is good enough at committing fraud, they could easily
understand what they need to do to not be detected, at least for long enough that they can get away with it before the customer would notice
and file a report themselves.

7. Precision or recall - While precision is important for this model since predicting too many positives will be a large
inconvenience for customers constantly getting fraud alerts, high recall is ultimately what the model should be aiming for. With something as serious 
as fraud it is definitely preferred that the model is able to find all cases of fraud, even if it is wrong sometimes. Customers would most likely agree that 
it is better to get ten fraud alerts where only one of them is real than to get no alerts, but their money is stolen from them. There obviously must be 
some balance between the two, but recall is the clear priority. 


PART 3 - DATA/PREDICTIONS


8. Poor data quality - Some data quality issues could be that historical, and even new data, might not have enough
of a transaction history to establish a good median, so the model would have trouble identifying what is abnormal. Also, if the data does not update properly,
a customer moving home addresses could mess with the predictions with the 'distance from home' feature showing many high abnormalities where there are actually
none. Finally, the model should identify that an online purchase may mean that a far distance from home or the last transaction should be void from consideration
because the customer could be ordering from their home but it is coming from far away. 

9. Overfitting the model - Similar to the previous question, if the model does not consider how each data point affects the other data
points, it will likely overfit. The online order being connected to the distance features is one area, and the median price being connected to distance from
home is another. With an online order, the distance features are likely to shoot up, and if the customer is on vacation (high distance from home) the transaction
prices will also likely shoot up. 

10. Can the results be trusted - Like previously stated, the most important factor to trust this model's predictions is the level of recall it can achieve. High
recall means the model is identifying as many fraudulent transactions as possible and so it is successful in its goal. The only danger with this model is, it has 
a low amount of features to truly be used as a real world model for an actual bank. There are so many more factors to consider, the ones in this one are just core
features that are likely to be the most important. 













